Specification TransactionRegister

Description The transaction register of one account: what each line shows, what
\ order the lines are in, and what the running balance means.
Details
\ A register is always a view of one account. Every line of it is one
\ transaction to that account, with the other side of the transaction
\ summarised in the Category column. A transaction with four transactions
\ appears once in each of the four registers, showing a different
\ amount and a different category each time, and it is the same
\ transaction.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A line of the register
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes RegisterLine
Description One line as it appears on screen.
Details Payment and Deposit are headed differently for different account types;
\ see the column heading rule below. Balance is the running balance after
\ this line.
| Attribute | Type           | Default    |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber    | none       |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none       |
| Category  | Text           | none       |
| Memo      | Text           | none       |
| Tag       | Text           | none       |
| Payment   | Money          | 0.00       |
| Deposit   | Money          | 0.00       |
| Cleared   | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared  |
| Balance   | Money          | 0.00       |

Scenario A register of simple transactions
Description The Category column holds the other account of the transaction. The
\ running balance is the balance of this account after each line.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary      | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | Acme Payroll  | Income:Salary      | 3000.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00  |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -52.00  |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Category           | Payment | Deposit | Balance |
| T1  | 2024-01-01 | Acme Payroll  | Income:Salary      | 0.00    | 3000.00 | 3000.00 |
| T2  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00   | 0.00    | 2955.00 |
| T3  | 2024-01-20 | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 52.00   | 0.00    | 2903.00 |

Scenario The same transaction seen from the other account
Description T2 above, in the register of the category rather than the bank. The
\ amount is on the other side and the category column names the bank.
\ Nothing was stored twice to make this possible.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary      | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | Acme Payroll  | Income:Salary      | 3000.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00  |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shell         | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | -52.00  |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Category   | Payment | Deposit | Balance |
| T2  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | [Checking] | 0.00    | 45.00   | 45.00   |
# In an Expense register the money arriving is what was spent, so it falls in
# the Deposit column. The column heading rule below gives it a better name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Column headings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule What the two amount columns are called
Description The register is read as a statement of the account it is open on, so
\ the headings are the words that account uses.
Examples: ColumnHeadings
| AccountType | OutColumn | InColumn | Notes                             |
| Checking    | Payment   | Deposit  |                                   |
| Cash        | Spend     | Receive  |                                   |
| CreditCard  | Charge    | Payment  | A payment reduces what is owed    |
| OtherDebt   | Increase  | Payment  |                                   |
| OtherAsset  | Decrease  | Increase |                                   |
| Brokerage   | Payment   | Deposit  | Of the cash side; see Investments |
| Expense     | Refund    | Spent    | Money arriving here was spent     |
| Income      | Earned    | Returned |                                   |
| Equity      | Decrease  | Increase |                                   |

Attributes ColumnHeadings
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| AccountType | AccountType | Checking |
| OutColumn   | Text        |         |
| InColumn    | Text        |         |
| Notes       | String      | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The running balance
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule The running balance uses the display sign of the account
Description A credit card register shows what is owed, growing as it is charged,
\ because that is what the statement shows. See the display sign rule
\ in Accounts.
Examples: RunningBalanceSign
| AccountType | PostingAmount | BalanceShown | Notes                      |
| Checking    | -45.00        | -45.00       | Money left the account     |
| CreditCard  | -45.00        | 45.00        | A charge; 45.00 is owed    |
| CreditCard  | 300.00        | -300.00      | A payment, on its own      |
| Income      | -3000.00      | 3000.00      | Earned                     |

Attributes RunningBalanceSign
| Attribute     | Type        | Default |
| AccountType   | AccountType | Checking |
| PostingAmount | Money       | 0.00    |
| BalanceShown  | Money       | 0.00    |
| Notes         | String      | none    |

Scenario A credit card register shows a growing balance owed
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path              | Type       | Placeholder |
| Liabilities:Visa  | CreditCard | false       |
| Assets:Checking   | Checking   | false       |
| Expenses:Shopping | Expense    | false       |
| Expenses:Dining   | Expense    | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee    | Destination       | Amount |
| T1  | Liabilities:Visa | 2024-03-02 | Amazon   | Expenses:Shopping | -89.99 |
| T2  | Liabilities:Visa | 2024-03-09 | The Cafe | Expenses:Dining   | -18.50 |
| T3  | Liabilities:Visa | 2024-03-25 | Visa     | Assets:Checking   | 100.00 |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine
| Ref | Date       | Payee    | Category          | Payment | Deposit | Balance |
| T1  | 2024-03-02 | Amazon   | Expenses:Shopping | 89.99   | 0.00    | 89.99   |
| T2  | 2024-03-09 | The Cafe | Expenses:Dining   | 18.50   | 0.00    | 108.49  |
| T3  | 2024-03-25 | Visa     | [Checking]        | 0.00    | 100.00  | 8.49    |
# A category keeps its full path; a real account -- the payment to the card --
# is shown in brackets with the root dropped.
# Payment here is headed Charge, and Deposit is headed Payment.

BusinessRule What order the register is in
Description By date, and within a date by the order the transactions were entered.
Details Entry order is used for the tie rather than amount or payee because it
\ is stable: reopening the file, or importing more, never reshuffles
\ lines that are already there, so the running balance against a line
\ does not change under the user.
Examples: RegisterOrder
| Date       | EntrySeq | Position | Notes                              |
| 2024-01-15 | 2        | 1        | Earliest date first                |
| 2024-01-20 | 1        | 2        | Entered first, dated later         |
| 2024-01-20 | 3        | 3        | Same date, entered after           |
| 2024-01-20 | 4        | 4        |                                    |

Attributes RegisterOrder
| Attribute | Type    | Default    |
| Date      | Date    | 2024-01-01 |
| EntrySeq  | Integer | 0          |
| Position  | Integer | 0          |
| Notes     | String  | none       |

Scenario Two transactions on one date keep the order they were entered in
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions in entry order are : SequencedTransaction
| EntrySeq | Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount |
| 1        | T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 |
| 2        | T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 |
| 3        | T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | -30.00 |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Payee  | Payment | Deposit | Balance |
| T2  | 2024-01-15 | Shop B | 20.00   | 0.00    | -20.00  |
| T1  | 2024-01-20 | Shop A | 10.00   | 0.00    | -30.00  |
| T3  | 2024-01-20 | Shop C | 30.00   | 0.00    | -60.00  |
# CompareOnly because this scenario is about the order of the lines. Without it
# the columns it does not name are asserted to be their defaults, and Category
# would have to be empty -- which it is not, and should not be.

Attributes SequencedTransaction
Description A transaction with the order it was entered in, so that a scenario
\ about ordering can say what that order was.
| Attribute     | Type           | Default    |
| EntrySeq      | Integer        | 0          |
| Ref           | TransactionRef |            |
| Date          | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee         | PayeeName      | none       |
| SourceAccount | AccountPath    |            |
| Destination   | AccountPath    | none       |
| Amount        | Money          | 0.00       |

Scenario Sorting the register by another column hides the running balance
Description A running balance down a list that is not in date order is a column
\ of meaningless numbers. Sorting by payee is useful for finding
\ something, so it is allowed, and the balance column is emptied
\ rather than filled with figures that do not add up.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions in entry order are : SequencedTransaction
| EntrySeq | Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount |
| 1        | T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 |
| 2        | T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 |
| 3        | T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | -30.00 |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column |
| Payee  |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Payee  | Balance |
| T1  | Shop A | 0.00    |
| T2  | Shop B | 0.00    |
| T3  | Shop C | 0.00    |
Then running balance is shown : BalanceColumnState Vertical
| Shown  | false                                  |
| Reason | The register is not in date order      |

DataType SortColumn
Description Which column the register is sorted by.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value   | Notes                                  |
| Date    | The default, and the only one that adds up |
| Payee   |                                        |
| Category |                                       |
| Amount  |                                        |
| CheckNo |                                        |
| Cleared |                                        |
| Memo    |                                        |
| Tag     |                                        |

Attributes BalanceColumnState
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Shown     | Boolean | true    |
| Reason    | Text    | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two balances at the top of the register
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description The top of the register states what the account comes to, what the
\ part of it the bank has seen comes to, and what the bank last said
\ it came to.
Details Reconciling is comparing two numbers, and until now only one of them
\ was on the screen. The other was on the bank's website, in another
\ window, and the difference had to be worked out in one's head
\ against a figure that had already scrolled away.
\
\ Three figures, because two of them answer different questions.
\ Balance is every transaction in the account, including the cheque
\ written this morning that the bank has not seen -- it is what the
\ money actually is. Cleared counts only what is marked cleared, which
\ is what the bank should agree with, and it is the one the downloaded
\ figure is compared against. Comparing the bank against Balance would
\ show a difference every time something was in flight, which is most
\ of the time, and a warning that is usually on is not read.
\
\ Downloaded is the balance the last download stated and the date it
\ was for; see what a download teaches an account in ImportOfx, which
\ is where it is written. It is shown as it was stated, with its date,
\ because a balance without a date cannot be compared with anything.
\
\ Difference is Cleared less Downloaded, and zero is the ordinary case
\ -- it means the import agreed, and it goes on being true for the
\ rest of the month if nothing is marked cleared by hand. Anything
\ else is the number to go looking with.

Attributes RegisterBalances
Description The three figures across the top of one register, in the display
\ sign of the account, the same as the running balance column. Never
\ stands in for the date when no download has reached the account, and
\ Downloaded and Difference are then both zero and are not shown.
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| Account      | AccountPath |         |
| Balance      | Money       | 0.00    |
| Cleared      | Money       | 0.00    |
| Downloaded   | Money       | 0.00    |
| DownloadedAt | Text        | never   |
| Difference   | Money       | 0.00    |

Scenario The top of the register shows both balances and the difference
Description A download agreed with the book at the end of January, and a cheque
\ written in February has not reached the bank. The three figures say
\ so: the account is down by the cheque, the bank is not, and nothing
\ is wrong.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | DownloadedBalance | DownloadedBalanceAt |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | 1705.00           | 2024-01-31          |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | 0.00              | never               |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | Opening       | Expenses:Uncategorized | 1750.00 | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00  | Cleared   |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-02-03 | Plumber       | Expenses:Uncategorized | -200.00 | Uncleared |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register balances are : RegisterBalances Vertical
| Account      | Assets:Checking |
| Balance      | 1505.00         |
| Cleared      | 1705.00         |
| Downloaded   | 1705.00         |
| DownloadedAt | 2024-01-31      |
| Difference   | 0.00            |
# Cleared and Downloaded agree, which is the point: the 200.00 is in the
# register and not at the bank, and that is a difference between Balance and
# Cleared rather than a disagreement with anybody.

Scenario A cleared balance that disagrees with the bank is a difference
Description The usual cause is a transaction marked cleared that the bank has
\ not seen, which is the same thing the import reports; this is where
\ it is still visible three weeks later.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | DownloadedBalance | DownloadedBalanceAt |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | 1705.00           | 2024-01-31          |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | 0.00              | never               |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | Opening       | Expenses:Uncategorized | 1750.00 | Cleared |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00  | Cleared |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Plumber       | Expenses:Uncategorized | -100.00 | Cleared |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register balances are : RegisterBalances Vertical
| Account      | Assets:Checking |
| Balance      | 1605.00         |
| Cleared      | 1605.00         |
| Downloaded   | 1705.00         |
| DownloadedAt | 2024-01-31      |
| Difference   | -100.00         |
# A hundred the register thinks the bank has seen and the bank does not. The
# figure is shown rather than a word, because the figure is what finds it.

Scenario A register of an account never downloaded for shows what it works out
Description Which is every account of a book imported from Quicken, so it is
\ the ordinary case rather than the exception. Nothing is missing from
\ the top of the register; there is simply nothing to compare.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type     | Placeholder | DownloadedBalance | DownloadedBalanceAt |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       | 0.00              | never               |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense  | false       | 0.00              | never               |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-01 | Opening       | Expenses:Uncategorized | 1750.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00  | Uncleared |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register balances are : RegisterBalances Vertical
| Account      | Assets:Checking |
| Balance      | 1705.00         |
| Cleared      | 0.00            |
| Downloaded   | 0.00            |
| DownloadedAt | never           |
| Difference   | 0.00            |
# Cleared is zero because nothing is cleared, not because the account is empty,
# and Difference is zero rather than 1705.00 because there is nothing to
# differ from. A number made up out of never would read as a problem.

Scenario A credit card register shows both balances the way the statement reads
Description The display sign applies to all three, because a card balance of
\ 450.00 owed compared against negative 450.00 is a comparison nobody
\ can make at a glance. See the display sign rule above.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder | DownloadedBalance | DownloadedBalanceAt |
| Liabilities:Visa       | CreditCard | false       | 450.00            | 2024-01-31          |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       | 0.00              | never               |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount    | Date       | Payee     | Destination            | Amount  | Cleared |
| T1  | Liabilities:Visa | 2024-01-10 | Amazon    | Expenses:Uncategorized | -450.00 | Cleared |
When register opened on : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register balances are : RegisterBalances Vertical
| Account      | Liabilities:Visa |
| Balance      | 450.00           |
| Cleared      | 450.00           |
| Downloaded   | 450.00           |
| DownloadedAt | 2024-01-31       |
| Difference   | 0.00             |
# The posting is negative 450.00 and all four figures read 450.00, which is
# what the statement says is owed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sorting by a column other than the date
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule What each column sorts on
Description Every column heading sorts the register, and what a column sorts on
\ is the thing the column means rather than the text it happens to show.
Details Amount is the one this matters most for. A register sorted on the
\ printed text puts 9.99 after 100.00, because that is alphabetical
\ order, and the person who clicked the heading was looking for the
\ largest payment. Amount sorts on the signed number, so the biggest
\ payment is at one end and the biggest deposit at the other, and the
\ Payment and Deposit columns are two views of that one number rather
\ than two sorts.
\
\ Date is the only order in which the running balance means anything,
\ so it is the only one that keeps the balance column; see the rule
\ above. Sorting back to Date restores it.
\
\ A column with nothing in it on some lines -- a check number, a memo --
\ puts the empty ones together at the start of an ascending sort. They
\ are not scattered through the list and they are not dropped.
Examples: SortKey
| Column   | SortsOn                        | Notes                                                  |
| Date     | The date, then the entry order | The default, and the only order the balance adds up in |
| Payee    | The payee name, ignoring case  | So that AMAZON and Amazon are together                 |
| Category | The destination as it is shown | Split sorts as the word, since that is all there is    |
| Amount   | The signed amount, as a number | Not the printed text; 100.00 is above 9.99             |
| CheckNo  | The check number, as a number  | 7 is above 100, and blank ones first                   |
| Cleared  | Uncleared before cleared       | The ones still to reconcile come to the top            |
| Memo     | The memo text, as it reads     | Blank ones first, like the check number               |
| Tag      | The tag field, ignoring case   | The field, not the words in it, so one sort per column |

Attributes SortKey
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Column    | String |         |
| SortsOn   | String |         |
| Notes     | String | none    |

Scenario Sorting by amount orders the numbers, not the printed text
Description Three transactions whose alphabetical order and whose numeric order
\ differ. Ascending puts the largest payment first, because a payment
\ is the most negative signed amount.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -9.99   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -100.00 |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-12 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | 20.00   |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column | Direction |
| Amount | Ascending |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Payee  | Payment | Deposit |
| T2  | Shop B | 100.00  | 0.00    |
| T1  | Shop A | 9.99    | 0.00    |
| T3  | Shop C | 0.00    | 20.00   |

Scenario Sorting by cleared brings the unreconciled lines to the top
Description What the column is for. Reconciling means working through the
\ lines the bank has not reported yet, and they are easier to find
\ together.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 | Uncleared |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-12 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | -30.00 | Cleared   |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column  | Direction |
| Cleared | Ascending |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Payee  | Cleared   |
| T2  | Shop B | Uncleared |
| T1  | Shop A | Cleared   |
| T3  | Shop C | Cleared   |
# The two cleared lines keep the order they were already in; see the tie rule.

Scenario Sorting by the same column twice reverses it
Description The heading is a toggle, which is what every list in every program
\ does. The second click does not sort by something else and it does
\ not clear the sort.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-12 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | -30.00 |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column | Direction  |
| Payee  | Descending |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Payee  |
| T3  | Shop C |
| T2  | Shop B |
| T1  | Shop A |

Scenario Sorting back by date brings the running balance back
Description The balance is emptied by a sort, not thrown away, so returning to
\ date order shows it again with the same figures as before.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column | Direction |
| Payee  | Ascending |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column | Direction |
| Date   | Ascending |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Payee  | Balance |
| T1  | 2024-01-10 | Shop A | -10.00  |
| T2  | 2024-01-11 | Shop B | -30.00  |
Then running balance is shown : BalanceColumnState Vertical
| Shown | true |

Scenario A sort leaves tied lines in the order they were already in
Description The entry-order tie rule is not only about dates. Two lines a sort
\ cannot tell apart stay in the order they were in, so clicking a
\ heading never reshuffles anything the person is looking at.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions in entry order are : SequencedTransaction
| EntrySeq | Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount |
| 1        | T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-20 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -10.00 |
| 2        | T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -20.00 |
| 3        | T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-18 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | -30.00 |
When register sorted by : SortBy
| Column | Direction |
| Payee  | Ascending |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Payee  |
| T2  | Shop A |
| T3  | Shop A |
| T1  | Shop A |
# Date order first, then the sort: the lines were in date order when the sort
# was asked for, and a sort that cannot tell them apart keeps them that way.

BusinessRule Which way round a sort starts
Description The first click on a heading sorts the way the column is most
\ useful, and the second reverses it.
Details Date starts ascending, oldest first, because that is the order the
\ register is already in and a first click on Date should look like no
\ change at all rather than a jump to the far end of the file. Amount
\ starts ascending as well, which puts the largest payment first; a
\ person sorting by amount is looking for a big one.
Examples: SortStart
| Column  | FirstClick | WhatThatShows                            |
| Date    | Ascending  | Oldest first, as the register already is |
| Payee   | Ascending  | A to Z                                   |
| Amount  | Ascending  | The largest payment first                |
| Cleared | Ascending  | Uncleared first, ready to reconcile      |

Attributes SortStart
| Attribute     | Type   | Default |
| Column        | String |         |
| FirstClick    | String |         |
| WhatThatShows | String |         |

