# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Date ranges with names, and a book that hides its early years
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule The date ranges the register offers by name
Description A range the user picks by name rather than by typing two dates.
\ Each one is worked out from today, every time it is applied.
Details A migrated book holds twenty years. The register that opens on it is
\ not useful at that length, and typing two dates to see this year is
\ work the program can do.
\
\ The names are worked out from today rather than stored, so a book
\ left open overnight into January shows the new year the next time
\ the range is applied. Nothing is recomputed behind the user: the
\ range in force is the one they chose, and it is applied again when
\ the register is reopened.
\
\ All dates is a named range too, and it is the one that is in force
\ when no range has been chosen. That keeps a register with no filter
\ and a register filtered to everything the same thing.
\
\ A named range is a filter, so the rule above holds: the running
\ balance against a line is the balance of the account and not of the
\ list, and an opening balance line carries what came before.
Examples: NamedDateRange
| Name           | From                            | To    | Notes                                    |
| All dates      | The first transaction            | Today | What a register with no filter shows     |
| This year      | The first of January this year   | Today | Calendar year, not fiscal                |
| Last year      | The first of January last year   | The last of December last year | A whole year, closed |
| Past two years | Two years back from today        | Today | Rolling, so it moves with today          |
| Year to date   | The first of January this year   | Today | The same range as This year, named as a report names it |
| Since a date   | A date the user types            | Today | What the two-date filter already does    |

Attributes NamedDateRange
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Name      | String |         |
| From      | String |         |
| To        | String |         |
| Notes     | String | none    |

Scenario This year shows the calendar year, not the fiscal one
Description The register is a list of what happened, and the user reading it
\ means the year on the calendar. The fiscal year start is a reporting
\ decision and does not move this range; see the fiscal year rule in
\ Reports.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given the book setting is : BookSetting Vertical
| FiscalYearStart | 7 |
Given today is : Today Vertical
| Date | 2024-05-15 |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee  | Destination        | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2023-12-20 | Shop A | Expenses:Groceries | 1000.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Shop B | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00  |
| T3  | Assets:Checking | 2024-02-20 | Shop C | Expenses:Groceries | -52.00  |
When register filtered to named range : NamedRangeSelect Vertical
| Name | This year |
Then register lines are : RegisterLine CompareOnly
| Ref | Date       | Balance |
| T2  | 2024-01-15 | 955.00  |
| T3  | 2024-02-20 | 903.00  |
Then opening balance line is : OpeningLine Vertical
| AsAt    | 2023-12-31 |
| Balance | 1000.00    |

